{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":65547507,"ns":0,"title":"Culturomics (microbiology)","revisions":[{"revid":1360148888,"parentid":1359002248,"minor":false,"timestamp":"2026-06-19T15:14:17Z","size":4902,"sha1":"29428b48c8e7cbc514e86731b27c70d2d5ce3c6e","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"'''Culturomics''' is the [[High throughput biology|high-throughput]] [[cell culture]] of [[bacteria]] that aims to comprehensively identify [[bacterial strain|strains]] or species in samples obtained from tissues such as the [[Human digestive system|human gut]] or from the [[Natural environment|environment]].<ref name=\"Lagier2012\">{{cite journal |vauthors=Lagier J, Armougom F, Million M et al |title=Microbial culturomics: paradigm shift in the human gut microbiome study |journal=Clinical Microbiology and Infection |date=December 2012 |volume=18 |issue=12 |pages=1185–1193 |doi=10.1111/1469-0691.12023|doi-access=free |pmid=23033984 }}</ref><ref name=\"GG\">{{cite journal |last1=Greub |first1=G. |title=Culturomics: a new approach to study the human microbiome |journal=Clinical Microbiology and Infection |date=December 2012 |volume=18 |issue=12 |pages=1157–1159 |doi=10.1111/1469-0691.12032|doi-access=free |pmid=23148445 }}</ref> This approach was conceived as an alternative, complementary method to [[metagenomics]], which relies on the presence of [[Homology (biology)|homologous]] [[Genetic sequence|sequence]]s to identify new bacteria.<ref name=\"GG\" /> Due to the limited phylogenetic information available on bacteria, metagenomic data generally contains large amounts of \"microbial dark matter\", sequences of unknown origin.<ref>{{cite journal |vauthors=Rinke C, Schwientek P, Sczyrba A et al |title=Insights into the phylogeny and coding potential of microbial dark matter |journal=Nature |date=25 July 2013 |volume=499 |issue=7459 |pages=431–437 |doi=10.1038/nature12352|doi-access=free |pmid=23851394 |bibcode=2013Natur.499..431R |hdl=10453/27467 |hdl-access=free }}</ref> Culturomics provides some of the missing gaps with the added advantage of enabling the functional study of the generated cultures. Its main drawback is that many bacterial species remain effectively uncultivable until their growth conditions are better understood. Therefore, optimization of the culturomics approach has been done by improving culture conditions.<ref>{{cite journal |vauthors=Diakite A, Dubourg G, Dione N et al |title=Optimization and standardization of the culturomics technique for human microbiome exploration |journal=Scientific Reports |date=December 2020 |volume=10 |issue=1 |page=9674 |doi=10.1038/s41598-020-66738-8|pmc=7295790 |doi-access=free |pmid=32541790 |bibcode=2020NatSR..10.9674D }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |vauthors=Chang Y, Hou F, Pan Z et al |title=Optimization of culturomics strategy in human fecal samples |journal=Frontiers in Microbiology |date=17 December 2019 |volume=10 |page=2891 |doi=10.3389/fmicb.2019.02891| pmc=6927924 |doi-access=free |pmid=31921067 }}</ref>\n\nUnlike metagenomics, which relies on direct [[shotgun sequencing]] or [[16S_ribosomal_RNA|16S rRNA gene]] sequencing, culturomics is based on [[matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization]]–[[Time-of-flight mass spectrometry|time-of-flight]] (MALDI-TOF) [[mass spectrometry]].<ref name=\"GG\" /> However, culturomics also uses 16S RNA sequencing to identify new species.<ref>{{cite journal |vauthors=Diakite A, Dubourg G, Dione N et al |title=Extensive culturomics of 8 healthy samples enhances metagenomics efficiency |journal=PLOS ONE |date=21 October 2019 |volume=14 |issue=10 |article-number=e0223543 |doi=10.1371/journal.pone.0223543|doi-access=free |pmid=31634343 |pmc=6802823 |bibcode=2019PLoSO..1423543D }}{{Expression of Concern|doi=10.1371/journal.pone.0278361|pmid=36512561|https://retractionwatch.com/2022/12/13/plos-flags-nearly-50-papers-by-controversial-french-covid-researcher-for-ethics-concerns/ ''Retraction Watch''|https://retractionwatch.com/?s=Didier+Raoult ''Retraction Watch''|doi-access=free}}</ref>{{better source|date=February 2025}}\n\n==See also==\n*[[Genomics]]\n*[[Microbiota]]\n\n==References==\n{{reflist}}\n\n==Further reading==\n*{{cite journal |last1=Lagier |first1=Jean-Christophe |last2=Hugon |first2=Perrine |last3=Khelaifia |first3=Saber |last4=Fournier |first4=Pierre-Edouard |last5=La Scola |first5=Bernard |last6=Raoult |first6=Didier |title=The rebirth of culture in microbiology through the example of culturomics to study human gut microbiota |journal=Clinical Microbiology Reviews |date=January 2015 |volume=28 |issue=1 |pages=237–264 |doi=10.1128/CMR.00014-14|doi-access=free |pmid=25567229 |pmc=4284300 }}\n*{{cite journal |last1=Bilen |first1=Melhem |last2=Dufour |first2=Jean-Charles |last3=Lagier |first3=Jean-Christophe |last4=Cadoret |first4=Fréderic |last5=Daoud |first5=Ziad |last6=Dubourg |first6=Grégory |last7=Raoult |first7=Didier |title=The contribution of culturomics to the repertoire of isolated human bacterial and archaeal species |journal=Microbiome |date=December 2018 |volume=6 |issue=1 |page=94 |doi=10.1186/s40168-018-0485-5|doi-access=free |pmid=29793532 |pmc=5966928 }}\n\n[[Category:Omics]]\n[[Category:Microbiology techniques]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-09-22T07:24:15Z","lastrevid":1360148888,"length":4902}]}}